  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>The first things to buy for a new home are the items that keep you safe and protect your investment: smoke and carbon monoxide alarms, a fire extinguisher, a basic tool kit, water leak sensors and better locks. Comfort upgrades and organization come next, and furniture and decor can wait until you have lived in the space for a while. Here is a prioritized checklist I give every new homeowner, whether you just closed on a resale or finished your own build.</p>

<h2>Priority 1: Safety essentials for the first night</h2>
<p>Before the first box is unpacked, make sure your family is protected.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Smoke and CO alarms:</strong> New homes are typically built with hardwired smoke alarms, and carbon monoxide alarms where code requires them. Test every one on move-in day. In an older home, check the manufacture date on each alarm; many manufacturers recommend replacement after about ten years.</li>
<li><strong>Fire extinguishers:</strong> Keep one in the kitchen and consider others in the garage and on each floor. Learn how to use it before you need it.</li>
<li><strong>First aid kit:</strong> Move-in week means box cutters, heavy lifting and ladders. Have a stocked kit where everyone can find it.</li>
</ul>
<!--products:smoke_co,fire_extinguisher,first_aid|Safety essentials for move-in day-->
<p>While you are at it, find your main water shutoff, your electrical panel and your gas shutoff if you have one. Label the breakers if the builder or previous owner did not.</p>

<h2>Priority 2: Basic tools every homeowner needs</h2>
<p>You will hang curtain rods, tighten cabinet hinges and assemble furniture in the first week. A good starter kit saves a lot of trips to the hardware store.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A general tool set:</strong> Hammer, screwdrivers, pliers, adjustable wrench, tape measure, utility knife and a level cover most move-in tasks. A [[amz:tool_set|homeowner tool set]] is an easy way to get them all at once.</li>
<li><strong>A cordless drill/driver:</strong> The single most useful power tool you can own. A combo kit with an impact driver is a solid pick for furniture, shelving and mounting TVs.</li>
<li><strong>A stud finder:</strong> Heavy shelves, TVs and mirrors should go into studs, not just drywall anchors.</li>
</ul>
<!--products:tool_set,drill_combo,stud_finder|Starter tools for new homeowners-->
<p>If you plan to take on bigger projects, or you built the house yourself, our list of <a href="/essential-tools-for-owner-builders/">essential tools for owner-builders</a> goes further.</p>

<h2>Priority 3: Protect the house and what is in it</h2>
<h3>Water leak sensors</h3>
<p>Water damage is one of the most common and expensive problems homeowners face. Small battery-powered sensors placed by the water heater, under sinks, behind the washer, near the refrigerator's ice maker line and by the HVAC condensate pan can alert you before a slow leak becomes a big repair. Many connect to your phone. A few [[amz:water_leak|water leak sensors]] are cheap insurance.</p>
<h3>Locks and entry</h3>
<p>You never know how many keys are floating around from previous owners, real estate agents, builders or subcontractors. Rekey the exterior locks or install a [[amz:smart_lock|smart lock]] so you control who gets in, and can issue temporary codes for contractors or guests.</p>
<h3>A video doorbell</h3>
<p>A video doorbell lets you see who is at the door, keep an eye on deliveries and get alerts while you are away. Check whether your HOA has any rules about exterior devices.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Call your homeowners insurance agent after you install leak sensors, smart locks or monitored alarms. Some insurers offer discounts for protective devices, and it only takes a few minutes to ask.</p></div>

<h2>Priority 4: Comfort upgrades that pay off quickly</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Blackout curtains:</strong> Bedrooms in a new home often come with bare windows or builder-grade blinds. Blackout curtains help with sleep and can cut afternoon heat gain on west-facing windows, which matters in a Texas summer.</li>
<li><strong>LED bulbs:</strong> Swap any remaining incandescent or older bulbs for LEDs. They use less energy and last much longer, and you can choose a warmer or cooler color for each room.</li>
<li><strong>Weatherstripping and door sweeps:</strong> In a resale home, check exterior doors for gaps. Sealing them helps your HVAC and keeps bugs out.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you want to go further on energy savings, our post on <a href="/smart-home-features-to-add-during-new-construction/">smart home features to add during new construction</a> covers thermostats, lighting and monitoring that also work as upgrades in an existing home.</p>

<h2>Priority 5: Organization and important documents</h2>
<p>Closing generates a stack of paperwork you will need for years: the deed, title policy, closing disclosure, warranty documents, appliance manuals, survey and inspection reports. Keep them organized and protected.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A fireproof safe or document box</strong> for the deed, passports, birth certificates and backup drives.</li>
<li><strong>A label maker</strong> for the breaker panel, storage bins, shutoff valves and pantry shelves. It sounds small, but it saves a lot of guessing later.</li>
<li><strong>A home binder or folder</strong> for warranties, paint colors, filter sizes and contractor contacts.</li>
</ul>
<!--products:label_maker,fireproof_safe|Keep your new home and paperwork organized-->
<p>If you have a new construction home, also note your builder warranty deadlines. Our <a href="/new-construction-walkthrough-punch-list/">new construction walkthrough punch list</a> explains how to use the 11-month walkthrough to get issues fixed before coverage ends.</p>

<h2>What can wait</h2>
<p>New homeowners often feel pressure to furnish every room right away. Resist it. Live in the home for a few weeks and notice where you actually sit, where clutter piles up and where light comes in. You will buy better furniture and avoid returns. Put a little money aside instead for the first surprise repair, which almost always shows up in year one.</p>
<p>A quick first-month checklist:</p>
<ol>
<li>Test smoke and CO alarms; buy extinguishers and a first aid kit.</li>
<li>Locate and label shutoffs and breakers.</li>
<li>Rekey or replace exterior locks.</li>
<li>Place water leak sensors at every water source.</li>
<li>Change HVAC filters and note the size.</li>
<li>File for your homestead exemption with your county appraisal district if you are in Texas.</li>
<li>Organize closing documents and warranties.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Start with the safety and protection items this week, then work down the list as your budget allows. For long-term care, [[amz:books_home_maintenance|a good home maintenance guide]] is worth keeping on the shelf, and if you are still planning your build, the <a href="/free-training/">free training</a> and our <a href="/new-construction-inspection-checklist/">new construction inspection checklist</a> will help you get it right from the start.</p>
